220
00:15:51,000 --> 00:15:55,000
between two scouts This past weekend. everybody was having a blast

221
00:15:55,000 --> 00:15:59,000
until we were breaking up a physical fight with punching and a kick to the

222
00:15:59,000 --> 00:16:03,000
head- Thankfully no injuries, but it was kind of ugly. I really did

223
00:16:03,000 --> 00:16:07,000
try to have the scouts work this out, but it's not going to happen. The parents of

224
00:16:07,000 --> 00:16:11,000
one of the scouts was looking into pressing charges. The parent of the other scout

225
00:16:11,000 --> 00:16:15,000
is withdrawn from the troop. Things really kind of spiraled out of control

226
00:16:15,000 --> 00:16:19,000
and I think the parents are being irrational. They're dictating terms to the troop

227
00:16:19,000 --> 00:16:23,000
committee and threatening to take their sons out of the troop and encouraging other families to follow them.

228
00:16:23,000 --> 00:16:27,000
So two difficult situations,

229
00:16:27,000 --> 00:16:31,000
two difficult questions, but I think- and I want to give an answer

230
00:16:31,000 --> 00:16:35,000
that I think applies to both of them- How do we resolve

231
00:16:35,000 --> 00:16:39,000
serious misbehavior problems like these incidents reflect?

232
00:16:39,000 --> 00:16:43,000
I want to say that these types of incidents, in my experience

233
00:16:43,000 --> 00:16:47,000
over 30 years as being a scout master, have been thankfully

234
00:16:47,000 --> 00:16:51,000
rare and I've tried lots of different ways of handling them,

235
00:16:51,000 --> 00:16:55,000
and the advice I want to give you is based on that experience.

236
00:16:55,000 --> 00:16:59,000
think of this type of thing in the terms of first aid.

237
00:16:59,000 --> 00:17:03,000
okay, Just bear with me here for a second. So if a scout

238
00:17:03,000 --> 00:17:07,000
gets a scratch, alls, that we need to do is make sure it gets washed out

239
00:17:07,000 --> 00:17:11,000
and it gets a bandaid- No big deal, We treat the scratch

240
00:17:11,000 --> 00:17:15,000
and we move on right. If a scout is using his

241
00:17:15,000 --> 00:17:19,000
pocket knife and he slips and he slices open his finger, well, we're going

242
00:17:19,000 --> 00:17:23,000
to administer first aid and stabilize the situation, but we're going to

243
00:17:23,000 --> 00:17:27,000
get him to a doctor because we don't stitch up wounds right.

244
00:17:27,000 --> 00:17:31,000
We don't carry a suture kit with us, We don't do stitches. That's something

245
00:17:31,000 --> 00:17:35,000
that a doctor needs to do Now. once he's been to the doctor and he's been treated,

246
00:17:35,000 --> 00:17:39,000
once the wound is healed, he can return to participating actively

247
00:17:39,000 --> 00:17:43,000
in the troop again. So what I want to do is draw a parallel between

248
00:17:43,000 --> 00:17:47,000
that kind of first aid situation. Some misbehavior we can handle pretty

249
00:17:47,000 --> 00:17:51,000
simply, just like the scratch, wash it out, put a bandaid on it-

250
00:17:51,000 --> 00:17:55,000
no big deal, We treat it and we move on. Some misbehavior-

251
00:17:55,000 --> 00:17:59,000
serious, just like that wound- that requires stitches and it's

252
00:17:59,000 --> 00:18:03,000
not within our power or expertise necessarily to resolve

253
00:18:03,000 --> 00:18:07,000
serious behavior issues. When scouts seriously misbehave,

254
00:18:07,000 --> 00:18:11,000
they're handed over to their family to handle things. and

255
00:18:11,000 --> 00:18:15,000
once things have been handled, then the family can send them back to the troop

256
00:18:15,000 --> 00:18:19,000
and tell us that the behavior's been fixed. That's the basic,

257
00:18:19,000 --> 00:18:23,000
simple approach that I recommend to you.

258
00:18:23,000 --> 00:18:27,000
We're volunteer scouters. We're not medical doctors.

259
00:18:27,000 --> 00:18:31,000
We can treat scratches, but we don't stitch up wounds. In the same way,

260
00:18:31,000 --> 00:18:35,000
we can deal with some minor misbehavior, but we don't address serious

261
00:18:35,000 --> 00:18:39,000
misbehavior with corrective punishment. That's the responsibility

262
00:18:39,000 --> 00:18:43,000
of a scouts family. We can counsel and do what

263
00:18:43,000 --> 00:18:47,000
we do to help scouts live by the scout oath and law, but when they

264
00:18:47,000 --> 00:18:51,000
misbehave seriously, it's time for their family to take over

265
00:18:51,000 --> 00:18:55,000
and correct the situation. When parents send their sons to us,

266
00:18:55,000 --> 00:18:59,000
it is with the understanding, the very clear understanding,

267
00:18:59,000 --> 00:19:03,000
that their sons are going to obey the scout oath and law while they are in our

268
00:19:03,000 --> 00:19:07,000
care. We aren't there to fix serious

269
00:19:07,000 --> 00:19:11,000
behavior problems. Once again, that is a parent's

270
00:19:11,000 --> 00:19:15,000
responsibility, It's not the responsibility of a volunteer

271
00:19:15,000 --> 00:19:19,000
scouter. Now, happily, I have only had to deal with three or four

272
00:19:19,000 --> 00:19:23,000
serious fights in 30 years. Repairing things with

273
00:19:23,000 --> 00:19:27,000
the scouts is usually easy. They're usually able to calm down, shake

274
00:19:27,000 --> 00:19:31,000
hands and let bygones be bygones. Parents, they

275
00:19:31,000 --> 00:19:35,000
can be a little bit of a different story and I'm going to tell you

276
00:19:35,000 --> 00:19:39,000
you can't control the way that they're going to react, and if they want to make a federal case

277
00:19:39,000 --> 00:19:43,000
about things, there's no way you're going to stop that. It's

278
00:19:43,000 --> 00:19:47,000
unfortunate, but thankfully rare that things get out of

279
00:19:47,000 --> 00:19:51,000
hand like this. Theft is another problem that I've had to deal with, but

280
00:19:51,000 --> 00:19:55,000
thankfully a extraordinarily rare occurrence.

281
00:19:55,000 --> 00:19:59,000
If something shows up missing and it's pretty clear that it was

282
00:19:59,000 --> 00:20:03,000
taken rather than misplaced- which you know is a whole other kettle

283
00:20:03,000 --> 00:20:07,000
of fish, making that determination and that you have to do on an individual

284
00:20:07,000 --> 00:20:11,000
basis, What I do is I speak to the scouts and

285
00:20:11,000 --> 00:20:15,000
I tell them that something is missing. and this is my turn to speak.

286
00:20:15,000 --> 00:20:19,000
It's my job as an adult leader to resolve serious situations

287
00:20:19,000 --> 00:20:23,000
like this. I may have the senior patrol leader standing right there with me

288
00:20:23,000 --> 00:20:27,000
and active in what's going on, but I'm going to take the lead on this.

289
00:20:27,000 --> 00:20:31,000
So I speak to the scouts, I tell them something is missing, that it's

290
00:20:31,000 --> 00:20:35,000
unlikely it was lost, but that someone had indeed taken

291
00:20:35,000 --> 00:20:39,000
it, and I tell them two things are now going to happen. When I finish

292
00:20:39,000 --> 00:20:43,000
talking, I'm going to step away for 15 minutes. When I do,

293
00:20:43,000 --> 00:20:47,000
you're going to go back, look through your things and see if it's possible that somehow

294
00:20:47,000 --> 00:20:51,000
the missing item is in your things. If you find it,

295
00:20:51,000 --> 00:20:55,000
you can hand it to me and I will return it to the owner.

296
00:20:55,000 --> 00:20:59,000
Now that's the first thing that's going to happen. If, after 15 minutes,

297
00:20:59,000 --> 00:21:03,000
the items aren't returned, well, we're going to do another 15 minutes. We could

298
00:21:03,000 --> 00:21:07,000
do what we came here to do, or we could spend the rest of the day looking for this missing thing.

299
00:21:07,000 --> 00:21:11,000
it's kind of up to you, scouts. So once we've gone

300
00:21:11,000 --> 00:21:15,000
through this and we have the missing items, or once we've stopped the

301
00:21:15,000 --> 00:21:19,000
fight and things have calmed down, the next time I'm going to spend time talking

302
00:21:19,000 --> 00:21:23,000
to the scouts involved. their parents are going to be present- And you know,

303
00:21:23,000 --> 00:21:27,000
if we're on a weekend camping trip, that could be the next day or something like that-

304
00:21:27,000 --> 00:21:31,000
and I will simply tell them. I said I'm expecting you to be on your best

305
00:21:31,000 --> 00:21:35,000
behavior from now until then, but the next time that we're going to sit

306
00:21:35,000 --> 00:21:39,000
and talk about this, your parents are going to be with us. So when they come to pick you up,

307
00:21:39,000 --> 00:21:43,000
you need to tell them they need to stay and we need to have a little bit of a discussion

308
00:21:43,000 --> 00:21:47,000
here. So when I meet with the parents, the scout in

309
00:21:47,000 --> 00:21:51,000
front of me and with their parents explains what happened.

310
00:21:51,000 --> 00:21:55,000
I tell the parents that the scout is permitted to continue participating

311
00:21:55,000 --> 00:21:59,000
in scouts once they can assure me that

312
00:21:59,000 --> 00:22:03,000
this sort of behavior won't be repeated. but until then,

313
00:22:03,000 --> 00:22:07,000
no meetings, no events. he needs to go home with his parents.

314
00:22:07,000 --> 00:22:11,000
they need to sort this out. they need to tell me it's sorted out. then he's back,

315
00:22:11,000 --> 00:22:15,000
no problem. Now parents could get upset and offended,

316
00:22:15,000 --> 00:22:19,000
but usually if I can get everybody to calm down and think, we do

317
00:22:19,000 --> 00:22:23,000
fine and this works. If parents want to bull you around or demand

318
00:22:23,000 --> 00:22:27,000
that this or that happen or dictate terms to the troop committee about

319
00:22:27,000 --> 00:22:31,000
what they think should happen, my advice is to show them

320
00:22:31,000 --> 00:22:35,000
the door. if that's your attitude and the actions that you want to take,

321
00:22:35,000 --> 00:22:39,000
that's just not something that we do in scouting. thanks for your time.

322
00:22:39,000 --> 00:22:43,000
there's the door. Now. I am giving very

323
00:22:43,000 --> 00:22:47,000
broad advice. it's very, very simple. I'm not going into

324
00:22:47,000 --> 00:22:51,000
a whole lot of detail in the content of the discussions and things like

325
00:22:51,000 --> 00:22:55,000
that. I don't think it's going to be very helpful. of course you're going to

326
00:22:55,000 --> 00:22:59,000
approach all of this in the best scout spirit and the best attitude.

327
00:22:59,000 --> 00:23:03,000
no doubt this type of things can make you really angry,

328
00:23:03,000 --> 00:23:07,000
it can make you disappointed, but you have to calm your own emotions and you have to deal

329
00:23:07,000 --> 00:23:11,000
with it and, like I said, this is very broad advice based on a few

330
00:23:11,000 --> 00:23:15,000
principles that are easy to remember. one is

331
00:23:15,000 --> 00:23:19,000
is that when it comes to serious misbehavior, we don't try to

332
00:23:19,000 --> 00:23:23,000
resolve serious misbehavior. parents do that. they're the ones who need

333
00:23:23,000 --> 00:23:27,000
to deal with their son. they can apply corrective punish if they see fit.

334
00:23:27,000 --> 00:23:31,000
we're not going to be dealing with that and all I need is a word

335
00:23:31,000 --> 00:23:35,000
from them that this is resolved. we spoke with our son,

336
00:23:35,000 --> 00:23:39,000
we've corrected the behavior and we consider that he is able

337
00:23:39,000 --> 00:23:43,000
now to begin participating in scouting again and that he will

338
00:23:43,000 --> 00:23:47,000
obey the scout oath and law while he's with you. we're not going to have

339
00:23:47,000 --> 00:23:51,000
any kind of set suspension. we're not going to have any kind of quasi

340
00:23:51,000 --> 00:23:55,000
judicial proceeding. we hand them over to his parents and then

341
00:23:55,000 --> 00:23:59,000
they hand them back to us when they consider that things are taken care of.

342
00:23:59,000 --> 00:24:03,000
the scout law has to mean something. it can't be a scout is trustworthy.

343
00:24:03,000 --> 00:24:07,000
but no, a scout is trustworthy.

344
00:24:07,000 --> 00:24:11,000
full stop period at the end of the sentence now, when you're 11 to

345
00:24:11,000 --> 00:24:15,000
17 years old, you get a break your brain and your character

346
00:24:15,000 --> 00:24:19,000
is fully cooked. you make sometimes very serious

347
00:24:19,000 --> 00:24:23,000
mistakes and bad choices. as much as we'd like to try,

348
00:24:23,000 --> 00:24:27,000
we can't save our scouts from making bad choices.

349
00:24:27,000 --> 00:24:31,000
we can't save them from making serious mistakes. we can't adopt

350
00:24:31,000 --> 00:24:35,000
extreme protective measures that absolutely assure that these

351
00:24:35,000 --> 00:24:39,000
things will never happen. when we are functioning based on trustworthy,

352
00:24:39,000 --> 00:24:43,000
we're vulnerable. that's just the way that it is, and everybody needs to understand

353
00:24:43,000 --> 00:24:47,000
that. what we can do when we are dealing with

354
00:24:47,000 --> 00:24:51,000
difficult or serious misbehavior, we react with

355
00:24:51,000 --> 00:24:55,000
fairness, compassion and empathy. we'll get it right or as close

356
00:24:55,000 --> 00:24:59,000
to right as is humanly possible. my best advice

357
00:24:59,000 --> 00:25:03,000
again is avoiding any kind of quasi law enforcement

358
00:25:03,000 --> 00:25:07,000
mindset. as I've said many times, we don't prosecute,

359
00:25:07,000 --> 00:25:11,000
we don't punish. we make parents aware of what's going on.

360
00:25:11,000 --> 00:25:15,000
we let parents do their job. now, if you find yourself in a situation like

361
00:25:15,000 --> 00:25:19,000
this, you're going to be disappointed, you could be

362
00:25:19,000 --> 00:25:23,000
furious or you're going to be discouraged. I can tell you that it gets better.

363
00:25:23,000 --> 00:25:27,000
that sounds right, but it's true. you simply have to rise to

364
00:25:27,000 --> 00:25:31,000
the occasion and base how you respond on the scout oath

365
00:25:31,000 --> 00:25:35,000
and law. it is actually that simple. I want to be clear

366
00:25:35,000 --> 00:25:39,000
that what you've heard is my best advice based on my experience.

367
00:25:39,000 --> 00:25:43,000
this isn't in any way an expression of official policies

368
00:25:43,000 --> 00:25:47,000
or procedures or anything like that. this is just the way

369
00:25:47,000 --> 00:25:51,000
that I have learned to handle these types of situations over time.

370
00:25:51,000 --> 00:25:55,000
I hope it helps. Scott Green- who incidentally is no relation

371
00:25:55,000 --> 00:25:59,000
is with Troop 1000 in Richmond, Texas, and Scott

372
00:25:59,000 --> 00:26:03,000
wrote in to say: I'm stepping in as the new scout master for a very active troop that's

373
00:26:03,000 --> 00:26:07,000
relatively large and well run. after listening to so many of your wonderful podcasts,

374
00:26:07,000 --> 00:26:11,000
I've gleaned the following truisms.

375
00:26:11,000 --> 00:26:15,000
one: scouting overall, and our troops specifically, is full of traditions.

376
00:26:15,000 --> 00:26:19,000
many are good, some not so much. two:

377
00:26:19,000 --> 00:26:23,000
improvements, such as better patrol method implementation, are needed in our troop.

378
00:26:23,000 --> 00:26:27,000
three, change can be stressful and is best

379
00:26:27,000 --> 00:26:31,000
done incrementally. four, change should preferably be pushed

380
00:26:31,000 --> 00:26:35,000
by the scouts rather than the adults. I've been thinking about how to merge

381
00:26:35,000 --> 00:26:39,000
all of those concepts. my plan is to challenge each senior patrol

382
00:26:39,000 --> 00:26:43,000
leader, shortly after he's elected, to do two things during his tenure.

383
00:26:43,000 --> 00:26:47,000
one is to pick out a tradition or method that we use

384
00:26:47,000 --> 00:26:51,000
and question it. why do we do it? should we continue doing it or not?

385
00:26:51,000 --> 00:26:55,000
my second is to start a new tradition that improves the way that we do

386
00:26:55,000 --> 00:26:59,000
things. my hope is to help each understand what it's like to have some

387
00:26:59,000 --> 00:27:03,000
long-term vision and to learn from your personal troop history and your personal

388
00:27:03,000 --> 00:27:07,000
perspective. what are your thoughts on this? your advice is always appreciated.

389
00:27:07,000 --> 00:27:11,000
well, Scott, welcome to the volcano that is being a scout master.

390
00:27:11,000 --> 00:27:15,000
the biggest challenge for all of us is empowering scouts to

391
00:27:15,000 --> 00:27:19,000
run their own troop. everything else follows on that simple change and

392
00:27:19,000 --> 00:27:23,000
attitude and perspective. I'll argue a little bit about your thought that change

393
00:27:23,000 --> 00:27:27,000
is stressful and is best done incrementally. I'll say that change

394
00:27:27,000 --> 00:27:31,000
is easy and instantaneous. it's adapting to change that

395
00:27:31,000 --> 00:27:35,000
causes stress and takes time, and I'm going to say probably most of

396
00:27:35,000 --> 00:27:39,000
the stress will come from your fellow adult volunteers. scouts

397
00:27:39,000 --> 00:27:43,000
usually adapt pretty easily and quickly to change. with that

398
00:27:43,000 --> 00:27:47,000
in mind, don't do change incrementally. do an instantly

399
00:27:47,000 --> 00:27:51,000
and then help everybody catch up. I like the idea of getting scouts to raise questions

400
00:27:51,000 --> 00:27:55,000
about how things are done. I suggest that it's a little bit more than a two-point

401
00:27:55,000 --> 00:27:59,000
plan. it's just kind of a new way of doing business. everything gets

402
00:27:59,000 --> 00:28:03,000
changed, not just one or two that you pick out. why are we doing this?

403
00:28:03,000 --> 00:28:07,000
is this working? should we try something else? that's the stock

404
00:28:07,000 --> 00:28:11,000
and trade set of questions for every scout master. it's what you'll ask

405
00:28:11,000 --> 00:28:15,000
all the time, because by asking those questions like broken record,

406
00:28:15,000 --> 00:28:19,000
you'll reveal not only what needs to change

407
00:28:19,000 --> 00:28:23,000
but how and why things work. knowing how and why things

408
00:28:23,000 --> 00:28:27,000
work is how you train your youth leadership, by getting them to think their way through,

409
00:28:27,000 --> 00:28:31,000
rather than just habitually repeating the things that have always been done,

410
00:28:31,000 --> 00:28:35,000
thoughtlessly. what the scouts do, the content of their activities, is going to remain

411
00:28:35,000 --> 00:28:39,000
pretty much the same. you're going to be modifying approaches.

412
00:28:39,000 --> 00:28:43,000
be careful not to throw the baby or the good stuff that the

413
00:28:43,000 --> 00:28:47,000
troop is doing right now out with the bathwater and think that everything

414
00:28:47,000 --> 00:28:51,000
has to change. and five years from now, what you'll agree on is that

415
00:28:51,000 --> 00:28:55,000
we cover the same ground over and over again, but to our scouts,

416
00:28:55,000 --> 00:28:59,000
there's always new territory. it's not so much having new and innovative

417
00:28:59,000 --> 00:29:03,000
activities and destinations and entertainments. it's about focusing

418
00:29:03,000 --> 00:29:07,000
on unique experiences. each individual scout has

419
00:29:07,000 --> 00:29:11,000
doing them. if you visited my troop 30 years ago, you'd see we're doing

420
00:29:11,000 --> 00:29:15,000
the same sorts of things and scouts are having the same

421
00:29:15,000 --> 00:29:19,000
sorts of experiences. there may be a minor alteration here and there, but it's

422
00:29:19,000 --> 00:29:23,000
pretty much the same stuff. in short, I think a good scout master keeps

423
00:29:23,000 --> 00:29:27,000
things simple. reduce your vision to as few words as possible,

424
00:29:27,000 --> 00:29:31,000
something like scouts become scouts to do some fun stuff with their buddies.

425
00:29:31,000 --> 00:29:35,000
they lead themselves. they do. what scouts do? we observe

426
00:29:35,000 --> 00:29:39,000
and facilitate. we achieve the aims of scouting. make it your

427
00:29:39,000 --> 00:29:43,000
job to understand the basics and you relentlessly focus on them.

428
00:29:43,000 --> 00:29:47,000
you're going to be successful. keep your eye on that and the details

429
00:29:47,000 --> 00:29:51,000
will follow. so, Scott, I hope that helps listen. if you have a question or a comment,

430
00:29:51,000 --> 00:29:55,000
you can get in touch with me and you're going to find out how to do that in just a moment.

